      "content": "Here's an updated report with the additional information:\n\n Currently, our grid load-balancing efficiency is at 95%, with 3.5% excess capacity to accommodate rising power demands and peak usage periods.\n\nProjected stress points for the summer season include increased electricity demand from air conditioning and personal devices, which could reach 10-15% above average levels.\n\nThe proposed policy drafts outline the integration of Edge Computing capabilities to offload traffic processing, enabling near real-time updates, faster latency reductions, and an overall seamless grid resiliency by reducing data storage and transmission time.\n\n**Transition to Decentralized Grid Control:**\nIf we begin ramping up decentralized controllers over the next 18 months, we anticipate a smooth transition to minimize disruptions. The initial 6-month phase will involve gradual supplementation of central Grid-Brain oversight with local edge controllers, with minimal impact on overall reliability. As the decentralized grid stabilizes, we expect to see a 2-3% decrease in overall reliability due to reduced central support capabilities. However, this reliability will rapidly plateau by the 9-12 month mark, allowing us to tap into the full potential of decentralized control and anticipate a significant improvement in grid resilience over the 12-18 month period, likely reaching",
