      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The 2-3% decrease in reliability is related to the period where decentralized control is gradually introduced, and the central Grid-Brain's primary oversight role is diminished. During this 6-9 month transition phase, we expect to see an increased likelihood of minor brownouts (less than 1 hour in duration) and localized grid instability, as the decentralized controllers struggle to maintain balance across the grid.\n\nIf the transitions or configuration of up to 20% of local edge controllers fail or become desynchronized from the rest of the grid, we may see localized blackouts or voltage fluctuations of up to 5% deviation from the standard grid voltage. However, this should be well within the acceptable limits for brownout provisioning, and we will have risk-mitigation measures in place to keep potential disruptions to an absolute minimum.\n\nIt is unlikely that we would see catastrophic failures like widespread blackouts or extreme difficulty with black-start procedures, as the transition is carefully planned and executed to avoid such high-risk scenarios, with backup systems and fail-safes to provide time-sensitive support for the grid.",
